  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>The town of Cogolin has installed its own dog washing station, the "Toutou Douche", right behind the municipal water sports centre, accessible from the car park at Les Marines de Cogolin beach. The idea: rinse your dog after the beach or a walk, without heading back to the car covered in sand. The town hall also presents it as water-saving: up to half as much as a wash at home.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>According to the town hall's official page (read on 31/10/2026), the rate is <strong>€1 a minute</strong>, paid by bank card or contactless from €1. Allow about 10 minutes for a medium-sized dog. How it works: first disinfect the shower (free), choose the duration, wet your dog with hot or lukewarm water, use the products supplied (shampoo, conditioner, anti-parasite treatment), rinse, then dry with adjustable intensity (gentle for short coats, stronger for long coats). The shower is at a good height and a ramp helps larger dogs step up. It can also be used simply to rinse paws after a walk.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: the town hall advises bringing a towel to cut drying time (and so the bill). According to the Town (page "Plage des Marines de Cogolin, été 2026"), the beach is open to dogs only from 7pm to 1am: the Toutou Douche is therefore especially handy after an evening swim or a walk around the neighbourhood.</div>

      <p>Getting there: the Marines beach car park is paid from 15 June to 15 September (8am-8pm); a free shuttle links the town centre to Les Marines from 6 June to 31 August 2026, where small dogs under 10 kg are accepted on laps or in a basket. Waste-bag dispensers are found at Les Marines (place Cortesi, rue Paul Arêne, Marines exit). The town hall page gives no opening hours for the shower: check before you go by calling the town hall, especially out of season.</p>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The Marines de Cogolin area, with its marina and beach, is part of the Gulf of Saint-Tropez. It's a natural stopover if you're heading down towards Saint-Tropez or Grimaud with your dog.</p>
